theory.intro:
If you know what a fractional part of a whole is worth, you can work back to find the whole. This reverses finding a fraction of a quantity.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=0.9cm,every node/.style={font=\small}]
\fill[blue!25] (0,0) rectangle (4,1);
\draw (0,0) rectangle (6,1);
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4,5} {\draw (\x,0)--(\x,1);}
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,amplitude=5pt}] (0,1.15)--(4,1.15);
\node[above] at (2,1.35) {known part};
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,mirror,amplitude=5pt}] (0,-0.15)--(6,-0.15);
\node[below] at (3,-0.35) {whole $=$ ?};
\node[above] at (5.5,1.05) {one part};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
$\frac{2}{5}$ of a collection is $8$.
Find the whole.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm,y=0.8cm]
\fill[blue!25] (0,0) rectangle (2,1);
\draw (0,0) rectangle (5,1);
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4} {\draw (\x,0)--(\x,1);}
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,amplitude=5pt}] (0,1.15)--(2,1.15);
\node[above] at (1,1.28) {$8$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.question_text:
$\frac{5}{8}$ of a tank is $35$ L. What is its capacity?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.75cm,y=0.75cm]
\fill[blue!25] (0,0) rectangle (5,1);
\draw (0,0) rectangle (8,1);
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4,5,6,7} {\draw (\x,0)--(\x,1);}
\draw[decorate,decoration={brace,amplitude=5pt}] (0,1.15)--(5,1.15);
\node[above] at (2.5,1.28) {$35$ L};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
